I have enough context to select the most relevant specialist archetypes for this diff. This is a large Python port of the Phase 5 PR/merge/logging segment of `ship-pr.sh` spanning ~3650 diff lines across 8 new modules and updates to existing ones.

{"archetypes":[{"name":"bash-parity","focus_area":"correctness","weight":2,"rationale":"The core risk is semantic drift between the Python ports and the original Bash scripts across merge classification, flush recovery predicates, OOS counting, and Mermaid sanitization.","prompt_body":"Audit the Python ports in python/merge.py, python/oos.py, python/pr_body.py, and python/run_logs.py for semantic parity with their Bash originals (merge-pr.sh, oos-disposition-gate.sh, sanitize-mermaid-fragment.sh, refresh-run-logs.sh). Focus on: (1) the four flush-commit recovery predicates in _flush_recoverable — subject prefix exact match including trailing space, count ≤ 5 cap, larch-logs/-only path check, and ancestor guard; (2) the OOS non-security block counter _count_non_security_markdown vs the awk block counting original; (3) the Mermaid fence parser and frontmatter detection in sanitize_fragment vs the shell original; (4) UNKNOWN/BEHIND retry counts (4 initial / 3 post-push) and any off-by-one in loop bounds. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"merge-state-machine","focus_area":"correctness","weight":2,"rationale":"merge.py is a complex stateful orchestrator with eight result literals, UNKNOWN retry loops, BEHIND early-exits, flush recovery, admin fallback, and version-race gate — all of which must compose correctly without silent short-circuits.","prompt_body":"Trace the full control flow of merge_pr in python/merge.py from the skip-mode guards through flush_logs_pre, the UNKNOWN retry loop, the _ensure_head_matches_pr branch, _version_race_gate, and _attempt_merge. Check for cases where _post_flush is called on some paths but not others (or called before error return vs after), whether the version-race gate correctly re-fetches and re-reads plugin.json (double-fetch) and whether the second fetch result is actually used, and whether admin-first in _attempt_merge correctly returns ADMIN_MERGED vs MERGED vs ADMIN_FAILED. Also check whether the return type annotation on _ensure_head_matches_pr (MergeResult | gh.MergeState | None) can lead to caller code in merge_pr silently ignoring the None branch without error. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"redaction-boundaries","focus_area":"security","weight":1,"rationale":"Multiple new outbound paths (PR bodies, issue comments, merge diagnostics, run logs) must all route through redact before reaching GitHub or disk — a single gap leaks secrets.","prompt_body":"Check every outbound write path that touches GitHub (gh.pr_edit_body, gh.issue_comment_patch, gh.issue_comment, tracking_issue upserts, pr_body composition) and every file write path in run_logs.py (_render_token_timing_batches, capture_session_transcript, _publish_run_tree_to_repo) for consistent redaction. Verify that redact.redact is applied before all gh writes, that the [content truncated marker fail-closed check is consistently applied (compare pr_body.py compose_pr_body vs update_pr_body), that issue_comment_patch in gh.py applies redact.redact to the body before JSON-serializing, and that run_logs token/timing batch writes apply redaction before copying to the repo larch-logs tree. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"idempotency-and-double-mutate","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"Silent double-merge or double-PR create are the highest-blast-radius bugs; ensure_pr and merge_pr must ground-truth probe before mutating.","prompt_body":"Audit ensure_pr in python/pr.py and merge_pr in python/merge.py for idempotency safety. In ensure_pr, check whether the existing-PR fast path verifies the PR is still OPEN (not MERGED or CLOSED) before pushing and updating the body. Check whether the new-PR path handles the race where two concurrent runners create the same PR (gh conflict recovery). In merge_pr, check whether the function re-probes mergeStateStatus after flush_logs_pre rather than trusting a stale value, and whether the admin-first _attempt_merge correctly falls back only when the admin call fails (not when it returns a non-200 HTTP transient). Also check test_merge_aborts_when_flush_skips_missing_state in test_merge.py — the test asserts flush_logs_pre skipped, but state_file=None should trigger state-file-missing-fail-closed, not a generic flush_logs_pre skipped message; verify the assertion is specific enough. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"path-traversal-slug","focus_area":"security","weight":1,"rationale":"New slug/path guards in run_logs.py gate log-commit paths — a bypass could write arbitrary files into the repo tree via the larch-log commit flow.","prompt_body":"Audit the path safety guards in python/run_logs.py: validate_run_id_slug, path_under_repo, and the _publish_run_tree_to_repo copy logic. Check whether validate_run_id_slug rejects all path-traversal forms including URL-encoded variants, whether path_under_repo correctly handles symlinks that resolve outside the repo root, and whether _publish_run_tree_to_repo could be made to copy an arbitrary src directory if effective_run_id returns a value that passes the slug validator but still escapes the expected prefix when joined with the tmpdir. Also check whether the shutil.copytree call in _publish_run_tree_to_repo follows symlinks in the source tree that could point outside the tmpdir. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"defer-push-rebase-parity","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"rebase.py was modified to add has_bump and defer_push parameters, threading base_remote/base_ref through apply_bump — wrong defaults or missing parameter threads could silently skip bumps or pushes.","prompt_body":"Audit the rebase_and_rebump changes in python/rebase.py for the has_bump=True and defer_push=False new parameters. Verify that the apply_bump call now correctly receives base_remote and base_ref (check version_bump.apply_bump's signature accepts these kwargs without error), that the conditional block under if has_bump correctly guards both classify_bump and apply_bump (not just one), that when has_bump=False the new_version stays None and no version-related git commits are made, and that when defer_push=True the function returns RebaseResult with pushed=False and no force-push is attempted. Also verify that the new validate_base_remote_ref call correctly fires before the rebase starts rather than after, and that a Stalled raise from it does not leave a dirty rebase state in progress. Check that unit tests cover has_bump=False and defer_push=True branches. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."}]}
